WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.958
Hi, I'm going to present CentriQ, a way to quantize diffusion transformers to 4-bit weights and 4-bit activations without any calibration data.

2
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:15.848
Quantizing both weights and activations to 4 bits is what actually speeds up a diffusion transformer, because its forward pass is compute-bound.

3
00:00:16.148 --> 00:00:26.709
But every method that reaches W4A4 on diffusion models today relies on calibration: smoothing factors, clipping ranges, rotations or low-rank corrections, fitted to prompts run through one specific checkpoint.

4
00:00:27.009 --> 00:00:33.476
For FLUX, SVDQuant's calibration took us thirteen hours, and it has to be redone for every model, fine-tune and bit-width.

5
00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.062
Without calibration, things fall apart.

6
00:00:37.362 --> 00:00:45.533
Plain round-to-nearest and HQQ produce pure noise on PixArt-Sigma, with an FID of 481, and lose the content of the prompt on FLUX and SANA.

7
00:00:45.833 --> 00:00:51.669
On the right you can see CentriQ, which stays close to the 16-bit output on all three models without seeing any data.

8
00:00:51.969 --> 00:00:53.245
So what breaks?

9
00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:59.462
Diffusion transformers condition every block through adaptive layer norm, which shifts each token by an offset.

10
00:00:59.762 --> 00:01:03.018
So every activation vector carries a per-token mean.

11
00:01:03.318 --> 00:01:10.186
The standard data-free fix for outliers is a Hadamard rotation, but a rotation preserves energy, and it maps this mean onto one or a few coordinates.

12
00:01:10.486 --> 00:01:15.857
That spike, shown here in blue, then sets the quantization step for the entire token.

13
00:01:16.157 --> 00:01:20.535
Remove it, and the 4-bit grid for this token becomes 2.4 times finer.

14
00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:33.833
Could a better rotation spread the mean evenly?

15
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:40.477
Only a regular Hadamard matrix, one with equal row sums, does that, and those exist only at orders four u squared.

16
00:01:40.777 --> 00:01:46.111
None of the evaluated widths, 1152, 2240 or 3072, has that form.

17
00:01:46.411 --> 00:01:54.735
On the right: rotation alone leaves the per-token max-to-RMS ratio where it was, while centering before rotation brings it down to what incoherence predicts.

18
00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:57.697
CentriQ does three things.

19
00:01:57.997 --> 00:02:02.976
It subtracts each token's mean, which costs order n and is computed in the same kernel launch as the scale.

20
00:02:03.276 --> 00:02:09.888
It rotates the centered token with a fixed factorized Hadamard matrix and quantizes it with one closed-form scale per token.

21
00:02:10.188 --> 00:02:18.409
And because the layer is linear, it adds the mean back exactly, as a rank-1 term, the mean times W times one, in full precision inside the GEMM epilogue.

22
00:02:18.709 --> 00:02:20.392
The mean never touches a quantizer.

23
00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:29.113
Here is one quantized layer.

24
00:02:29.413 --> 00:02:35.931
The online path centers, pads and rotates each token, quantizes it to INT4 and runs the INT4 GEMM.

25
00:02:36.231 --> 00:02:39.040
The orange branch carries the mean around both quantizers.

26
00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:45.481
Offline, the weights are fitted under a robust l-p objective with p equal to 0.7, which we call RoDS.

27
00:02:45.781 --> 00:02:52.133
Because the marginal cost of a large residual shrinks, the dense mode of each weight group sets the scale, not its heavy tail.

28
00:02:52.433 --> 00:02:53.913
Neither path uses data.

29
00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:58.803
Here are the main results at W4A4 on MJHQ.

30
00:02:59.103 --> 00:03:08.850
Across PixArt-Sigma, FLUX and SANA, CentriQ matches calibrated SVDQuant on FID and ImageReward and has the lowest LPIPS against the 16-bit images, without using any data.

31
00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:17.967
Because SVDQuant's numbers come from its paper, we also regenerated it under our exact protocol: the two match on FLUX, and CentriQ is ahead on PixArt-Sigma.

32
00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.716
Here is what that looks like.

33
00:03:21.016 --> 00:03:24.263
Every image in a row shares the prompt and the initial noise.

34
00:03:24.563 --> 00:03:32.985
On PixArt-Sigma, CentriQ stays close to the 16-bit wolf, while HadaNorm-style centering and rotation alone, run in the same setting, break the image.

35
00:03:33.285 --> 00:03:38.163
On FLUX, CentriQ and calibrated SVDQuant are both close to the 16-bit image.

36
00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:54.945
Speed.

37
00:03:55.245 --> 00:04:00.723
Both 4-bit methods run in the same fused Nunchaku engine, so they differ only in the quantizer.

38
00:04:01.023 --> 00:04:12.473
CentriQ is within 3.5 percent of SVDQuant's latency on an A100, and within 2.8 percent on an RTX PRO 6000, where its transformer is 1.76 times faster than eager BF16.

39
00:04:12.773 --> 00:04:18.823
Converting a new checkpoint takes 9.3 minutes for FLUX, against thirteen hours of calibration, with about the same peak memory.

40
00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:20.949
Below four bits.

41
00:04:21.249 --> 00:04:31.595
On FLUX, CentriQ is the first calibration-free method to keep usable image quality at 2-bit activations, and it outperforms the strongest calibration-free method reported so far at 2-bit weights.

42
00:04:31.895 --> 00:04:42.329
The ablation on the right shows why: rotation helps, centering closes the gap at four bits, and a single shared clip percentile brings W4A2 from an FID of 444 down to 36.

43
00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:49.596
To sum up: the per-token mean that adaptive layer norm adds is what breaks data-free rotations on diffusion transformers.

44
00:04:49.896 --> 00:04:55.760
CentriQ centers each token, rotates, and restores the mean exactly, so it never touches a quantizer.

45
00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:08.222
With no calibration data, it matches calibrated SVDQuant at W4A4 on three models, runs within 3.5 percent of its latency, and is the first calibration-free method with usable quality at 2-bit activations.

46
00:05:08.522 --> 00:05:12.327
The paper is on arXiv, and the code and project page are coming soon.

47
00:05:12.627 --> 00:05:13.588
Thanks for watching.
